<ToolLayout title="Message Queue Patterns Reference" description="Compare point-to-point, pub-sub, dead letter queue, priority queue, and delayed message patterns.">
  <QueuePatternsReference client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why queue pattern choice shapes your whole architecture</h2>
  <p>Point-to-point and pub-sub solve fundamentally different problems — distributing work across workers versus broadcasting events to multiple independent consumers — and choosing the wrong one early can force awkward workarounds later as your system grows.</p>

  <h2>How to use this reference</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Determine whether you need work distributed once (point-to-point) or broadcast to multiple consumers (pub-sub).</li>
    <li>Add dead letter queues and priority handling as needed for reliability and urgency requirements.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Not implementing a dead letter queue</strong>, leaving repeatedly failing messages to either block a queue indefinitely or be silently dropped.</li>
    <li><strong>Using pub-sub when point-to-point was actually needed</strong>, causing multiple workers to duplicate the same processing work.</li>
    <li><strong>Ignoring message ordering guarantees</strong> — not all queue systems guarantee strict ordering, which matters for some workflows and not others.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Designing an event-driven architecture between microservices</li>
    <li>Choosing a queue pattern for distributing background job processing</li>
    <li>Adding resilience (dead letter queue) to an existing message processing system</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Can I combine multiple patterns?</strong> Yes — many real systems use pub-sub for event broadcasting alongside point-to-point queues for work distribution, plus dead letter queues layered on both.</p>
</ToolLayout>
